  "slug": "evolutionary-rescue-adaptation-0377",
  "summary": "New stickleback, Boechera and wild-population genomic studies test which genetic features predict adaptation, persistence and erosion under environmental change.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is which genomic and population features determine whether populations adapt or persist under environmental change. Icelandic stickleback adapted to geothermal warming show distinct genetic architectures for within- and transgenerational plasticity. Replicated introductions across nine lakes indicate that source population identity predicts success better than environmental matching. In Boechera stricta, genome-environment analyses suggest hot, dry projected climates may exceed adaptive capacity. Declining yellow-bellied marmots show recent Ne and census collapse after 2010 with elevated inbreeding, while a bird study indicates that temporal genomic erosion estimates need demographic and technical validation because estimator and degradation biases can arise. New here is the emphasis on source effects, separable plasticity architectures, and methodological limits when inferring persistence from genomic data.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
